Reading /bin/sh back into C - reconnaissance
============================================

Not yet written.  This is what has been established so far, so the next
pass starts from here rather than from the beginning.  Read
cmd/form/NOTES first - the Whitesmiths conventions described there
(frame helper, switch helper, how disas has to be told about them) all
apply, and form is the worked example.


Shape
-----

    disas filesystem/bin/sh

    whitesmiths type 94 symlen 9 norelocs
    symbols: 0 text: 100(883d) data: 9000(bee) bss: 0

34877 bytes of text against form's 13339, and stripped like everything
else.


What it is
----------

Not a Bourne shell.  The builtins include alias, unalias, source, dir,
era and prompt, the startup files are ~/.login and ~/.sh, and the
prompt is "# " - this is csh-flavoured.  man1/sh.1 is 443 lines and is
the specification; read it before writing any code.

Strings worth knowing:

    0111  "Syntax error."
    2227  "Missing name for redirect."
    17cd  "<>&|"              the metacharacters
    1bea  "/.login"           startup, relative to home
    1bf2  "/.sh"
    1c6a  "# "                the prompt
    1c70  "read" "-v" "-c"    flags
    1efa  "/usr/bin" "/bin"   the default path
    09fd  "/dev/null"
    490e  Terminated, Alarmed, Broken pipe, ... signal descriptions
    4b70  JTERM, ALRM, PIPE, SYS, ...        signal names

There is no message catalogue beyond that; as with form, a large part
of the image is library.


The builtin table
-----------------

The names are a packed list at 0x2c9e:

    unalias\0alias\0kill\0home\0path\0type\0source\0nice\0era\0dir\0
    sync\0echo\0pid\0prompt\0exit\0wait\0chdir\0cd\0

and the dispatch table is {char *name; int code;} records at 0x9755 in
DATA, eighteen of them:

    cd       1     echo     6     path    13
    chdir    1     sync     8     home    15
    dir      2     prompt   9     kill    16
    era      3     pid     10     alias   17
    wait     4     type    11     unalias 18
    exit     5     source  12     nice    19

cd and chdir share code 1 - they are synonyms.  Codes 7 and 14 are
absent from the table, so either the switch has cases reached some
other way or the numbering has gaps; that is worth resolving early,
since it may point at builtins that are recognised but not listed.

TWO TRAPS, both of which cost time here:

  1. The table is at ODD alignment (0x9755, and the records straddle
     odd addresses).  A scan for pointers that steps two bytes at a
     time walks straight past it.  Step one byte.

  2. The table is in DATA and the names are in TEXT.  Searching the
     text segment for a "LD HL,2c9e" that loads the list finds
     nothing, because nothing does - the pointer is an initialised
     variable, not an immediate.  Search both segments for the raw
     bytes before concluding anything.


The builtin handlers
--------------------

The switch table is at 0x075e, seventeen cases and a default, found
now that disas recognises the helper by signature:

    cd/chdir  1  H0817      pid      10  H09e7
    dir       2  H085c      type     11  H0990
    era       3  H08a1      source   12  H09f2
    wait      4  H09bb      path     13  H0979
    exit      5  H08b2      home     15  H08cb
    echo      6  H086d      kill     16  H08ba
    sync      8  H098a      alias    17  H07d9
    prompt    9  H09c6      unalias  18  H09a1
                            nice     19  H0908

    default      H0811

CODES 7 AND 14 ARE SIMPLY NOT USED.  They are absent from the name
table AND from the switch, so they are gaps in the numbering rather
than builtins recognised but unlisted.  That was the open question
in the first pass and it is now closed.

Seventeen distinct codes over eighteen names, because cd and chdir
are the same case.


Entry and main
--------------

    H0100   startup.  It calls TWO things:

                CALL H011f      main(argc, argv)
                ...
                CALL H7117      exit(status)

    H011f   MAIN.  Two arguments, argc at +4 and argv at +6; it keeps
            argc in 0x963e and argv in 0x961c.
    H7117   exit.
    H88e2   frame helper, and H88ed a second one - sh's equivalents of
            form's H34c0, at the head of every function.
    H85a3   a multiply, called to index arrays by a variable.

I first recorded H7117 as main because it is the second thing the
startup calls and it opens with a loop.  It is exit: the loop is the
stdio flush described below.  When two calls come out of the startup,
the first is main and the second is what is done with its result.

"Syntax error." sits at 0x0111, immediately after the call to main,
and disassembles as instructions - the tracer follows the call and
never comes back, so the string is never reached as code.  Do not
read the first few instructions after a call as if they were code
without checking whether they spell something.

The table at 0x99b0 is the STDIO STREAM TABLE - sixteen FILEs of
sixteen bytes.  Only three are initialised, and they say what the
layout is:

    [0]  +6=0001  +8=0000
    [1]  +6=0002  +8=0001
    [2]  +6=0006  +8=0002

+8 is 0, 1, 2 - the file descriptors, so these three are stdin,
stdout and stderr.  +6 is flags; bit 0 is set on the reading stream
and bit 1 on the two writing ones, so bit 1 is "writable" (the 6 on
stderr is bit 1 plus something else, probably unbuffered).  +0 and +4
are the buffer pointers: exit skips a stream when they are equal,
which is to say when there is nothing held.

    0x99b0  the stream table, sixteen bytes per FILE
      +0/+4 buffer pointers, equal when empty
      +6    flags, bit 1 = writable
      +8    file descriptor

exit's loop is therefore the flush: for each stream, skip it if the
buffer is empty, skip it unless it is writable, otherwise write it
out.  That is what fooled me into calling H7117 main.

    0x9ab0  the loop counter, and a general scratch byte
    0x9ab1  scratch word, the +0 minus +4 difference
    0x963e  argc          0x961c  argv

The syscall stubs are worth knowing, since they anchor everything:

    H8121  exit        H815a  getpid
    H8127  fork        H815f  getuid
    H813a  indir

Micronix syscalls are the CF trap ("SYS exit"), and disas decodes
them, so grepping the disassembly for SYS is the fastest way to find
where a routine actually touches the system.  Everything sh calls:

    16  indir           fork, wait, pipe, dup, close, nice, sync,
     1  each of         getpid, getuid, exit

The sixteen indir sites are the ones that matter - indir is how
Micronix passes a call whose arguments live in memory, so open, exec,
read and write are all in there.  Finding what each one dispatches is
the next concrete piece of work, and it maps the whole of how the
shell talks to the system.


The shell body
--------------

main does three things:

    H1c7b(argv, argc)       set up from the command line
    H015d()                 the read-eval loop
    H7117(*0x9008)          exit with the status kept in 0x9008

H015d allocates 0x40a bytes of locals - a line buffer among them, at
DE-0x208 - and loops:

    H0165   clear bit 5 of the flag word at 0x99c6
            if the word at 0x9002 is zero, skip the interactive
            checks and go straight to H01a0
            (0x9002 and 0x9008 are both initialised to 1)

    H01a0   H42b4()             per-iteration reset
            H424e()             the other half of it
            H2c12(buf)          read and parse one line
            if buf[0] is not zero, go to H01e6 and execute it

            otherwise nothing was read.  If the stack at 0x9646 is
            empty, leave the loop at H01fb - that is end of input.
            Otherwise pop a word off it, hand it to H5f06(), and go
            back to the top.

0x9646 is a stack pointer and 0x9648 its base; it pops by subtracting
two, and "empty" is tested as base minus pointer without borrow.  A
stack that is unwound when input runs out, in a shell whose builtins
include source, is the stack of input sources - so H5f06 is very
likely the routine that resumes the previous one.  That last step is
inference, not yet traced; everything above it is read off the code.

    0x9002  tested at the top of every iteration, initialised 1
    0x9008  the exit status main passes to exit
    0x99c6  flag word, bit 5 cleared each time round
    0x9646  input-source stack pointer, base 0x9648


The parser
----------

CORRECTION: an earlier note here called H2c12 "read and parse a line".
It only reads.  It is 137 bytes and its only real call is H6448, the
fgets of this libc.  The parsing is in the routines the loop calls
after it.

H015d calls fifteen routines.  By size, which is the quickest way to
see which ones matter:

    H2257  1465   the biggest thing in the shell
    H03f5   727
    H1339   687
    H5292   515
    H2057   461
    H02d6   278
    H56ab   196
    H5f06   156   resumes a popped input source
    H2c12   137   reads one line
    H4f0c   126
    H29ad    83
    H424e    54
    H2cfc    27   called from everywhere - a small common helper
    H42b4    16

and separately, not called from the loop directly:

    H17d7  1040   THE TOKENISER.  It owns the metacharacters.

The two copies of "<>&|" at 0x17cd and 0x17d2 are both inside H17d7,
used at two different points, and both times like this:

    H783d(*scan, "<>&|")

so H783d is the "is this character in this set" test, and 0x9bec is
the pointer being scanned along the line.  The shape at 0x1ab9 and
0x1ad0 is a word scanner: stop at a metacharacter, stop at the end of
the line, otherwise take the character and go on.

H2257 does NOT reference the metacharacters, so it is not the
tokeniser; it is the layer above.  It IS the parser proper, because
"Missing name for redirect." at 0x2227 is referenced from 0x27c8,
which is inside it.  So H2257 is where a command is built and where
its redirections are attached.

    H2257   parser: builds commands, attaches redirections
    H17d7   tokeniser: splits on "<>&|" and whitespace
    H783d   is-this-char-in-this-set, the test both use
    H16f2   19 bytes: print via H7cab, then exit.  A fatal error.
            H2257 calls it three times, so there are three ways for
            the parse to give up.
    H772d   string EQUALITY.  Compare two strings a character at a
            time, return 0 at the first difference, return 1 only
            when both reach the terminator together.  Not strcmp -
            there is no ordering in it.

H772d is called eight times from H2257 because there are eight
operators, and the parser tests the input against each in turn.  They
are stored packed at 0x2242, immediately BEFORE the parser that uses
them, in the order they must be matched - longest first, or ">" would
swallow ">>":

    |&   pipe, stderr as well        >>   append
    |    pipe                        >&   stdout and stderr to a file
    &    background                  >    stdout to a file
    >>&  append, stderr as well      <    stdin from a file

The forms ending in & are what make this a csh: they send the
standard error wherever the standard output is going.  There is no
here-document and no "2>".  That is the whole grammar.

"Syntax error." at 0x0111 is referenced from NOWHERE that disas can
see.  Either it is reached by an address the tracer does not resolve,
or it is dead.  Worth settling before writing the error paths, since
a message with no reference may mean a whole branch is missing from
the trace.


Inside the execution engine
---------------------------

H0a14 takes ONE argument, a pointer, and keeps it in 0x9bec.  It
takes sixteen bytes of locals, zeroes two of them, and then loops.
Each time round it tests

    the word at +0 of the node        zero ends the loop
    the field at +0x17                zero ends the loop
    the field at +0x17 against 0x13

0x13 is 19, which is B_NICE in the builtin numbering.  So the node
carries a BUILTIN CODE at +0x17 and nice is special cased in the
engine - which fits, since nice is the one builtin that then has to
run something else.

So the parser does not hand execution a flat array.  It builds nodes
with a code in them, and the engine walks a chain.  The C's
struct pipeline with its array of struct cmd is a flattening of that,
and under the call-graph goal it is the wrong shape.

These are the constant offsets added to pointers anywhere inside
H0a14.  Only +0 and +0x17 are CONFIRMED to be fields of the command
node; the rest are seen in the same routine and some certainly belong
to other structures, so this is a list to work from, not a layout:

    +0x00 +0x01 +0x02   +0x0a +0x0b +0x0c   +0x11 +0x13 +0x15
    +0x17 +0x19         +0x1f +0x21 +0x23

The pairs two apart - 0x17/0x19, 0x1f/0x21/0x23 - look like adjacent
words, which would make the node at least 0x25 bytes.  Worth settling
by finding where the parser ALLOCATES one: the size it asks for
answers this outright.


The execution subsystem
-----------------------

H0a14 is the top of it, 2043 bytes, and these are what it is built
from.  Confirmed by reading them, not by their size:

    H0a14  2043  the engine.  close x15, dup x8, dobuiltin x4,
                 ignoresigs x2, exit x3, runcmd x3
    H07a8   594  runs one command.  Twenty-four distinct calls, the
                 widest fan-out in the shell - this is where the
                 special cases live
    H5897   286  a SUBSHELL: it calls H015d, the read-eval loop,
                 and then exit.  So this is the child that runs a
                 script or a parenthesised list
    H57c4   208  redirection: two closes, an open through indir, and
                 H8228
    H1285   158  two string equalities and two messages
    H1228    77  builtin dispatch - holds the switch at 0x075e
    H48dc    47  builtin lookup: takes a word, searches with a
                 24 byte local
    H4287    42  IGNORE SIGNALS.  signal(2,1) and signal(3,1) -
                 SIGINT and SIGQUIT to SIG_IGN - through H827f,
                 which is therefore signal()
    H7e5f    24  execv(path, argv).  Its whole body is pushing two
                 arguments and calling the exec stub H8100

So the shape to match is: the engine forks, the child ignores
signals, sets up redirection, and either execs or becomes a
subshell; the parent waits.


THE GOAL: MATCH THE CALL GRAPH
------------------------------

The C does not have to be the same code, but it should have the same
FUNCTIONS in the same relationships.  One C function per function in
the binary, calling what that one calls.  Anything in the C with no
counterpart in the image is an invention and should go; any function
in the image with nothing in the C is a hole.

Where that stands, measured with tools/coverage.py:

    172 functions in the image, 169 below the first syscall stub
    33120 bytes of them
    13 functions identified, 4625 bytes - fourteen per cent

so the reconstruction currently matches about a seventh of it, and
the shape of the rest is unknown.  The libc floor used for that split
is crude - several of the larger unnamed routines at high addresses
are certainly library rather than shell - so fourteen per cent is a
floor, not a measurement.

The biggest holes, all shell rather than libc:

    H0a14  2043   THE EXECUTION ENGINE.  Bigger than the parser.
                  Calls close fifteen times and dup eight, the
                  builtin dispatch four, pre-fork setup twice.  The C
                  does this job in about a hundred lines across
                  redirect/spawn/runpipeline, so it is a heavy
                  simplification of something with far more
                  redirection machinery in it.
    H2dc8   844
    H03f5   732   called from the read-eval loop
    H1339   690   called from the read-eval loop
    H3d25   677
    H07a8   615   called three times from the execution engine
    H725f   527
    H5292   518   called from the read-eval loop
    H2a0e   516
    H2057   510   called from the read-eval loop

and 136 smaller ones, 15287 bytes in all.

Going the other way, what the C has that the image does not:

  - findcmd() searching a colon separated pathlist.  WRONG TWICE.
    The image keeps an array of three char * at 0x9741, laid out
    backwards like the builtin table, pointing at

        0x1f08 "."   0x1f03 "/bin"   0x1efa "/usr/bin"

    so the path is three entries and one of them is the current
    directory, which the C leaves out altogether.  Which end it
    searches from is not yet read.
  - startup() joining the home directory to "/.login" - the strings
    are real, the mechanism is a guess.
  - nextline() prompting only at the outermost input level.
  - the wait loop in runpipeline, and (status >> 8) & 0xff.
  - the close(2)/dup(1) for the & forms; the operators are real, this
    implementation of them is not traced.


Next
----

  - H1c7b is where -v, -c and "read" (0x1c70) are handled, and where
    ~/.login and ~/.sh (0x1bea, 0x1bf2) must be read from
  - H2c12 reads and parses a line and is the bulk of the program;
    "<>&|" at 0x17cd is where redirection and pipes are recognised
  - H01e6 is execution: fork, the exec at block 0x9b70, pipe and dup
  - confirm H5f06 really is "resume the popped input source"
  - sh.1 gives the whole language; the binary settles the details
